<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Bosnian translation.

    English, in `values/`, is the source language (SPEC §9); this file is a
    translation of it and is kept complete. The rules are the ones that hold
    everywhere: positional placeholders, <plurals> for anything that agrees in
    number, and the `name` attributes left untouched.

    The typography is Bosnian: quotation marks are „ … “, and the dash that
    breaks a sentence is – with a space on either side, not the em dash the
    English file uses. It replaces that dash everywhere — inside sentences
    (`welcome_data_body`, the four `journey_walk_*` lines) as well as inside
    the one format-only string that carries one, `city_label`. The only em dash
    left is `counterpart_none`, which is a glyph standing in for an absent
    figure rather than punctuation. Diacritics are written in full — č ć ž š
    đ — and never approximated by their bare letters. The apostrophe, where a
    network's name carries one (Vélib’), is ’ (U+2019) and never the straight
    quote, which is why nothing here is escaped.

    The reader is addressed with persiranje, the polite plural, which is what
    Android's own Bosnian does: the 5 900 system strings extracted from this
    phone's `framework-res.apk` and `Settings.apk` say Odaberite, Provjerite,
    Dodirnite and Instalirajte, and never the singular. Buttons take the second
    person singular imperative for the same reason — Nastavi, Otkaži, Izbriši,
    Preskoči, Prikaži, Zamijeni — which is Android's own habit for a control
    rather than a sentence. `action_retry` is *Pokušaj ponovo*, singular,
    because `settings:retry` is; the sentences that end on the same idea keep
    the polite *pokušajte ponovo*.

    It is Bosnian and not Croatian or Serbian, both of which are translated
    separately: sistem and not sustav, ekran, otkaži, nazad, tačka, šta, ko,
    indeks, fajl, meni, interfejs, historija, saradnik, dobija, brojali — each
    following the lexicon where the lexicon has a word, and Bosnian usage where
    it has none.

    The preposition is s and not sa, which is an orthographic rule the three
    standards share rather than a marker of any of them: sa stands only before
    s, š, z, ž, before mnom, and before a spelled-out abbreviation. The lexicon
    reads 60 bare s against 15 sa, and every one of the fifteen is before a
    sibilant or an abbreviation. Two lines here keep sa for that reason —
    journey_no_dock_nearby and, in the store text, sa svakom GBFS mrežom.

    Bosnian declines, and a case is a suffix. A sentence built around a
    placeholder has to stay right whatever the placeholder holds, so several
    lines here are turned around rather than given an ending they could not
    control: a name is set in apposition after a noun that carries the case, or
    the line becomes a label with a colon.

    Plural categories are Bosnian's three. What each covers is not its smallest
    number: `one` takes 1, 21, 31…, `few` takes 2, 3, 4, 22, 23, 24…, and
    `other` takes everything else — 0, 5 to 10, and 11 to 14.

    See docs/bs-glossary.md for the words this file holds to, and
    CONTRIBUTING.md to submit a translation.
-->
<resources xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

